## 3\. What Landed

### 3.-6 Stage 7 — End-of-Game perspective (state slot, not yet flow-triggered)

**Why now.** Building the visual STATES before wiring a game flow on top. The user explicitly wants to verify every UX state in isolation. EoG is a third top-level perspective alongside Defender/Attacker, manually selectable from the seg.

**Architecture.** Three top-level perspectives now: `Defender · Attacker · End of Game`. EoG is its own state — NO firing, NO aiming, NO assignment, NO probe-toggle, no shooting from any perspective via any handler. The `body[data-perspective="endgame"]` selector hides every defender-only and attacker-only chrome; new `.endgame-only` pane carries the EoG sidebar content.

**Sub-views inside EoG.** A pill toggle inside the EoG pane: `FROM ATTACKER` vs `FROM DEFENDER`. Same underlying truth, different emphasis. State held in `state.endgameView`.
| Board element | FROM ATTACKER | FROM DEFENDER |
|----|----|----|
| Probe history (X marks, miss dots) | full opacity, prominent | dimmed (\~45%) |
| Ship hulls + trajectories + wrecks | dimmed (\~50%) | full opacity, prominent |
| Sidebar | silhouette validation panel | fleet outcome panel |

**Sidebar panels:**

* `FROM ATTACKER` — silhouette validation: for each silhouette idx 0..4 show actual size (now revealed), did the player declare-sunk via assignment, was it actually sunk, verdict (`✓` correct / `✗` false positive / `?` missed an actual sink). Score line at top.
* `FROM DEFENDER` — fleet outcome: per ship card showing sunk-or-survived, hits taken, distance traveled (from `sim.history`), final heading.

**Gating audit (load-bearing — this is the user's explicit ask).** Every interaction has to be hard-disabled in EoG:

* Hex click handler: `probingEnabled` gate now requires `state.perspective !== "endgame"` (was a latent bug — flipping Probe in Defender then switching to EoG would let clicks fire `probeCell`).
* Silhouette card click → `toggleAssignmentMode`: card not rendered in EoG (panel is attacker-only). Belt-and-suspenders check inside the handler.
* `setAim` / `fireDirectSalvo`: already gate on `state.perspective === "attacker"`. Confirm.
* Spread picker, SHOT 1/2, teletype line: all attacker-only via existing perspective gating. EoG inherits that hiding automatically.
* `Escape` key: clears aim + exits assignment — harmless no-op in EoG.
* Step Forward/Back: KEPT functional in EoG. The user can scrub the sim and watch EoG update.
* Inference aid toggles (Hit aura, Contact tracks): UI is attacker-only, so hidden in EoG. State persists.

**Deferred to Stage 7b / when game flow lands:**

* Auto-trigger EoG when `fleet.filter(s => s.sunk).length === 5` (right now it's manual via the seg button).
* Animated reveal sequence (trajectory line draw, sequential ship reveals).
* Wrap-aware trajectory polylines.
* Click affordances inside EoG (hover wreck → see when, click silhouette → see truth highlight).

### 3.-5 Stage 6c — Silhouette ↔ Hit Assignment

**Why.** Once we decided the sunk reveal is end-of-game only (Stage 7), the player has nothing real-time to push their hypothesis onto. The silhouette panel was static decoration. 6c gives it teeth: the player commits attribution mid-game by tagging X marks to silhouettes. EoG reveal then validates those commitments against truth.

**Mechanic.**

* Click silhouette card body → toggle **assignment mode** for that silhouette. Card visually highlights with the silhouette's color.

* In assignment mode, click any hex on the board:

  * Hex has hit entries → toggle assignment of all hit entries at that cell to current silhouette.
  * Hex has no hits → exit assignment mode.

* Escape → exit assignment mode.

* Click another silhouette → switch mode to it. Clicking an X that's tagged to silhouette A in B's mode re-tags it to B. **Moving X's between ships is implicit** — no special UI.

**Data.**

* `attacker.assignmentMode: number | null` — silhouette index currently being assigned (one at a time).
* `hitLog[i].attackerAssignedSilhouette: number | null` — per-entry tag. Renderer reads this to tint the X mark.
* `attacker.declaredSunk` — kept for manual "hunch" path (existing behavior preserved via a small skull-toggle on each card).

**Visual.**

* Each silhouette card shows: size + `M of N assigned` + a small skull toggle for manual declared-sunk + the cells visual (now tinted with silhouette color when count > 0).
* Tagged X marks render in the silhouette's color (overrides default red); age treatment opacity still applies.
* Enemy Fleet header shows total hit count: `2/5 confirmed sunk · 7 hits · 3 declared`.
* Visually "declared sunk" if EITHER manual flag OR `assignedCount ≥ size`. Dimmed + red ✕ overlay either way.

**Multi-hit cells.** Click on a cell with multiple hit entries → all entries on that cell flip together. Per-entry granularity deferred (rare case of two ships actually transiting one cell).

**Contact tracks interaction.** Assignment color on the X mark and contact-track line color on the connecting segments are independent. If they disagree, that's information for the player.

**Lifecycle.** `attacker.assignmentMode` and per-entry tags clear on resetSim / enterRunningMode / enterPlacementMode alongside the other attacker transient state.

## What Landed

### 3.-4 Stage 6 — Contact Tracks (§7.4) · MVP

* **Inference engine (`inferTrack(hits)`).** Given a set of HIT probes, exhaustively searches `(size ∈ {2,3,4,5}) × (heading 0..5) × (cooldown ∈ {1..5}) × (first-hit body-cell index)` and returns every quadruple whose trajectory places a body cell at every hit's (q,r) at every hit's tick. Torus wrap is folded in: anchor positions are computed in raw axial space and `wrapHex` is applied at the body-cell comparison step (any unwrapped equivalent matches). Straight-line motion only — no turning, no post-hit cooldown bump. Both deferred.

* **AUTO correlation (`computeContactTracks()`).** Greedy single-pass: process hits in tick order; for each new hit, try adding it to each existing track and pick the track whose feasibility set survives best (most constrained = most informative). If no track stays feasible, start a new one. **Inference IS the correlation criterion** — no separate distance/time heuristic.

* **Confidence labels.**

  * `1 HIT` — single-hit track, \~420 hypotheses, no real attribution.
  * `CONSISTENT` — exactly one hypothesis survives. Heading/cooldown/size known.
  * `FORCED` — multiple hypotheses survive but the set is constrained. UI shows the ambiguity count.
  * `INCONSISTENT` — no hypothesis survives. Track glows red on board + sidebar.

* **Sidebar Contacts panel** between Inference Aids and Teletype. One card per track with NATO phonetic name (Alpha/Bravo/Charlie…), hit count, inferred heading/cooldown/size (bold when narrowed to one, `N candidates` otherwise), confidence chip, and the hit list (`T2 (3,5) → T7 (4,5)`). Empty-state hint: `No contacts (toggle on to auto-correlate).`

* **On-board lines** (`#layer-contacts`) — thin colored segments between consecutive hits in each track. One color per track from `TRACK_COLORS`. Inconsistent tracks render red + thicker; `CONSISTENT` tracks are solid, others dashed. Segments where `hexDistance > state.radius` are skipped — those almost certainly cross the torus wrap and a straight line would be misleading.

* **Toggle**: `Auto contact tracks` in Inference Aids. CSS-driven visibility via `body[data-contact-tracks]`. When turning ON, we recompute since we don't maintain `attacker.contacts` while off (saves work in the common-off case). Reset alongside `declaredSunk` on lifecycle transitions.

### 3.-3 Stage 5 — Hit Aura (§7.3)

* **Toggle in a new `Inference Aids` sidebar section** between Enemy Fleet and Teletype. `state.showHitAura` (default `false` — it's an aid, not always wanted).
* **CSS-driven visibility** via `body[data-hit-aura="on/off"]`. Toggle handler just flips the body attribute — no `renderBoard` call, no DOM rebuild. Layer is always rendered, just hidden when off.
* **Aura layer position** in the layer stack: right after the vignette, before ships and markers. That way the X mark stays crisp on top of the soft aura ring.
* **Radius growth.** `auraR = hexR * max(1, 1 + age * 0.7)`. Fresh hit ≈ 1 hex; age 10 ≈ 8 hexes (≈ ⅓ of an R=8 board), matching the spec phrasing.
* **Stroke / opacity decay** with age too: `stroke = max(0.6, 2.0 - age * 0.08)`, `opacity = max(0.10, 0.32 - age * 0.012)`. Fresh rings are crisp and clearly red; old rings are wide, thin, and faint — natural "cloud" feel without a radial gradient.
* **Hits only, not misses.** A miss is *confirmed-empty* info, not uncertain ship-position info. Drawing aura on misses would suggest the opposite. (The aura is an inference aid about ship MOTION; a miss tells you nothing about where any ship currently is.)

### 3.-2 Stage 4 — Enemy Fleet Silhouette Panel (§7.5)

* **Silhouettes show sizes only**, never class names (R18). `ENEMY_SILHOUETTES` = `[{idx:0,size:5}, {idx:1,size:4}, {idx:2,size:3}, {idx:3,size:3}, {idx:4,size:2}]`. Player-side identity is "silhouette idx", not "ship id" — the attacker has no truth mapping from one to the other.
* **State.** `attacker.declaredSunk` is a `Set` of silhouette indices the player has marked. Cleared on `resetSim` / `enterRunningMode` / `enterPlacementMode`.
* **Header tally.** `<N confirmed sunk> / 5 [· M declared]`. Confirmed count reads `fleet.filter(s => s.sunk).length` — just the count, never *which* (per R18). Declared count is the size of the player's hypothesis set.
* **Render** via `buildEnemyFleetSilhouettes()` — DOM rebuild, called from `probeCell` (after every probe, so confirmed-count updates), lifecycle transitions, and `toggleDeclaredSunk()`. Each silhouette is a row: size label · inline SVG with N rounded-rect cell pips · status text. Declared-sunk rows dim, get an X overlay, and the left border turns red.
* **Click to toggle.** Click on any silhouette toggles its membership in `declaredSunk`. Non-destructive — a wrong guess can be reversed by clicking again. (The design's intent is that wrong hypotheses *cost* you turns chasing ghosts; the UI doesn't punish you, the game state does, but that emerges only once contact tracks in Stage 6 surface "this hit can't belong to a declared-sunk silhouette".)
* **Deferred to Stage 6:** the "Contact" state with hit-count pip (`2/?`). Requires hit→ship attribution from contact tracks, which doesn't exist yet. For now silhouettes are only "at large" or "declared sunk".
* **Deferred to Stage 7:** the "Confirmed (wreck icon)" reveal — that's part of the sunk-reveal animation flow which renders the trajectory across the attacker's hit history.

### 3.-1.5 Stage 3 redo — Firing UX, take 2 (click-click direct salvo)

Stage 3 v1 (bottom-center 4-pattern picker) tested badly: picker too far from click, gaps between buttons caused hover-flash, no rotation control, clicked-aim cell visually forgotten by the time you reached the picker. Scrapped the picker entirely.

* **No picker, no preset patterns.** The mechanic is *click-click*: first click sets the aim (golden reticle), second click on any other hex fires both as a salvo. The teletype line at the bottom of the board carries all the textual guidance.
* **Hover preview = the entire UX surface.** On mouseenter into any cell while aim is locked, the cell paints ghost-orange and the teletype updates live: `> TGT (3,5) + (3,4) · TIGHT PAIR · FIRE FOR EFFECT_`. Move the mouse to a different cell → preview + label refresh. There's nothing to navigate to.
* **Salvo label by hex distance** between the two clicked cells. `dist 1 → TIGHT PAIR`, `dist 2 → WIDE SPREAD`, `dist ≥ 3 → RANGE N`. Same `salvoLabelByDistance()` helper used by both the live teletype prompt and the Teletype console salvo header in the sidebar — one source of language.
* **Cancel** by clicking the aim cell again or pressing Escape.
* **Removed**: `SPREAD_PATTERNS`, `patternCells()`, `setPatternHover()`, `fireSalvo()`, `#spreadPicker` HTML/CSS, all picker button event handlers, the `patternKey` field on probe entries. Replaced by `attacker.hoverDestination` (single cell), `fireDirectSalvo(q, r)`, `setHoverDestination()`, mouseenter/leave on hex polygons.

### 3.-1 Stage 3 — Firing UX (§7.7)

* **Patterns.** Four spread patterns (`tight / line / wide / wedge`) defined in `SPREAD_PATTERNS` as axial offsets. Fixed N-axis orientation for v1; rotation is a follow-up if needed. `patternCells(aim, key)` wraps offsets onto the torus.

* **State.** New `attacker` object at module scope holds `aim` + `patternHover`. Cleared on `enterPlacementMode`, `enterRunningMode`, `resetSim`, and on `setPerspective("defender")`. Salvos numbered via a module-scope `nextSalvoId`, reset alongside.

* **Click flow.** In attacker, the hex click handler calls `setAim(q,r)` instead of probing. Clicking the same aim cell again unlocks (toggle). Switching perspective or pressing `Escape` clears.

* **Visual feedback.**

  * Aim reticle: golden double-ring + crosshair drawn at the aim cell.
  * Preview layer: ghost-orange polygons fill the cells the hovered pattern would probe — wraps over the torus.
  * Picker: 4 buttons (`TIGHT / LINE / WIDE / WEDGE`) in a row at the bottom-center of the board area; shows/hides via `body[data-aim="locked"]`.
  * Teletype line: just above the picker, monospace, with a blinking cursor — updates live as the user hovers patterns (`> TGT (q,r) · PATTERN: LINE · FIRE FOR EFFECT_`).

* **Salvo firing.** `fireSalvo(patternKey)` calls `probeCell()` once per cell with `{ salvoId, patternKey }` threaded through. Each probe still goes through the existing hit/miss/animation pipeline; clearAim runs after both cells resolve.

* **Teletype console.** Sidebar `Probe Log` is now `Teletype`. `updateProbeLog()` groups entries by `salvoId` (consecutive entries with the same id collapse into one block). Each salvo header reads `> SALVO N · T# · PATTERN [· REVISIT] [· VESSEL DESTROYED]`; each cell line reads `(q,r) HIT|MISS [(last T#)]`. Single-cell probes from the defender's Probe-toggle tool render as standalone `> PROBE · T#` blocks (no salvoId on those entries).

* **REVISIT detection.** Per entry, indexed once per `updateProbeLog` call — `O(N)` linear scan builds `lastVisitAt[i]`. Salvo headers say `REVISIT` if *any* cell in the salvo had a prior visit.

### 3.0 Stage 2 — Cell Visual Lifecycle (§7.2)

* **Age buckets** instead of a smooth gradient (per spec phrasing). Helpers `attackerHitAgeTreatment(age)` / `attackerMissAgeTreatment(age)` return a treatment object per bucket; rendering reads palette/opacity/font scale off it. Pure functions, module scope, just before section 5.
* Hits: `Fresh (this turn)` bold + animated · `Recent (1-5)` full saturation · `Aging (6-10)` slight fade · `Stale (>10)` desaturated red-brown, small label.
* Misses: never disappear — fade to a floor (`fillA: 0.18`, `lblOp: 0.30`).
* **Cell-history grouping** for attacker (§9.2 "a cell is not a state, it's a history"): entries grouped by `(q, r)`, sorted by tick. Primary = most recent event drives the marker. Up to 3 older events stack as small color-coded badges in the top-right corner; overflow shows `+N`.
* Defender side **unchanged** — defender markers visualize current damage, not historical timing. Defender doesn't get age fade.
* **Refactor:** extracted `renderFreshHitBurst(entry)` and `renderFreshMissSplash(missG, slot, entry)` as closures inside `renderBoard()`. Both attacker and defender paths call them for fresh events — removed \~150 lines of duplication risk for Stage 3+.

### 3.1 Perspective scaffolding

* `state.perspective = "defender" | "attacker"` (default `"defender"`).
* `setPerspective(p)` updates state, `body[data-perspective="..."]`, the seg control, the corner label, the meta text, then re-renders. Resets `sim.probeMode` on the way into Attacker.
* Sidebar gets a `Perspective` segmented control at the very top. Defender sections are wrapped in `<div class="defender-only">`; Attacker sections in `<div class="attacker-only">`.
* Visibility is **CSS-driven** by `body[data-perspective="..."] .defender-only / .attacker-only { display: none !important; }` — no JS show/hide churn.

